-- POST: may-tinh-cu-bao-hanh-6-thang-quyen-loi
UPDATE public.blog_posts SET
  title_en = 'Used Computers with a 6-Month Warranty: What Does It Cover, and How Is It Different from a 7-Day "Test Cover"?',
  excerpt_en = 'What does the uniform 6-month warranty on a used computer actually cover, which components are included, and how is it different from a 7-day "test cover"? Vi Tinh An Tam explains everything transparently so you can buy with confidence.',
  content_en = $html$<p>When you buy a used computer, the most important question is not "how much does it cost" but <strong>"if something fails, who is responsible — and for how long?"</strong> At Vi Tinh An Tam, every used computer — both pre-built desktops and business laptops — comes with a <strong>uniform 6-month full-system warranty</strong>, regardless of model or price. This article explains exactly what that warranty covers and how it differs from the 7-day "test cover" commonly offered on the used-computer market.</p>

<h2>What does the "uniform 6-month full-system warranty" mean?</h2>
<p><strong>Uniform</strong> means every machine we sell carries the same 6-month term — no "expensive machines get a longer warranty, cheap ones get a shorter one," and no haggling. <strong>Full-system</strong> means the warranty covers the hardware of the entire machine: for a PC, the case, mainboard, CPU, RAM, drive and power supply; for a laptop, the mainboard, CPU, RAM, drive, keyboard and screen.</p>
<p>The <strong>laptop battery</strong> is a wear-and-tear component, so we handle it differently — transparently: before a machine is sold, its battery is tested and its health is reported clearly (every machine we sell has a battery at 80% health or above). You see the actual figures first, then decide — instead of a token promise of a long-term battery warranty.</p>

<h2>Comparison: 6-month warranty vs. 7-day test cover vs. sold as-is</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Uniform 6-month warranty</th><th>7-day "test cover"</th><th>Sold as-is, no warranty</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Support period for hardware faults</td><td>6 months from the handover date</td><td>First 7 days</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>All of the machine's hardware</td><td>Usually only "won't power on" faults</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paperwork & lookup</td><td>Warranty slip included, plus <a href="/tra-cuu-bao-hanh">online lookup by serial number</a></td><td>Usually just a verbal agreement</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>If a fault appears after 1–2 months</td><td>Free repair or replacement with an equivalent component</td><td>You pay for it yourself</td><td>You pay for it yourself</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>In real-world use, most faults in used computers — degrading drives, faulty RAM, weak fans and power supplies — show up after a few weeks to a few months, well outside the 7-day "test cover" window. That is why a 6-month term is the milestone that genuinely protects the buyer.</p>

<h2>What happens if your machine develops a fault during the warranty period?</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Contact us and describe the problem</h3>
<p>Call the hotline <strong>0909.053.058</strong> or message us on Zalo, and include your machine's serial number. Many software issues and minor faults can be fixed remotely right away via UltraViewer, so there is no need to bring the machine in.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Inspection and hardware diagnosis</h3>
<p>If a hands-on inspection is needed, bring the machine to our shop at <strong>52 Nguyen Cuu Van Street, Long An Ward, Tay Ninh Province</strong> (or arrange a local pickup). Our technicians will inspect it and tell you clearly, before any work begins, whether the fault is covered by the warranty.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Repair or replacement with an equivalent component</h3>
<p>Hardware faults covered by the warranty are repaired, or the faulty part is replaced with an equivalent component, <strong>free of charge</strong>, with a clear estimated turnaround time — you are never left waiting indefinitely.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Handover and record update</h3>
<p>The machine is tested again before handover, and the repair history is saved to the machine's electronic record so future lookups stay transparent.</p>

<h2>What is not covered by the warranty</h2>
<p>To be clear from the start, the warranty does not cover: damage from drops, water ingress, fire, or unstable power supply; machines that have been opened or repaired elsewhere, breaking the inspection seal; and software problems caused by programs the user installed (in these cases we still help you fix them, with some work charged at our published service rates). All terms are stated in writing at handover — there are no hidden conditions.</p>

<h2>Why did Vi Tinh An Tam choose a uniform 6 months instead of "6–12 months depending on the machine"?</h2>
<p>Because a uniform term is <strong>easy to remember, easy to verify and fair</strong>: whether you buy a machine for VND 5 million or VND 15 million, you get exactly the same protection. The policy also forces us to source carefully — only machines that meet our standard are ones we dare to warranty for half a year. You can <a href="/tra-cuu-bao-hanh">check the warranty status</a> of any machine by its serial number right on our website, and browse the models currently on sale on the <a href="/#san-pham">products page</a>.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What does the 6-month warranty on a used computer include?</h3>
<p>It covers all of the machine's hardware (case, mainboard, CPU, RAM, drive and power supply; laptops also include the keyboard and screen): any hardware fault within 6 months is repaired, or the part replaced with an equivalent, free of charge. Laptop batteries are tested and their health — 80% or above — is reported clearly before you buy.</p>
<h3>What if the machine develops a fault after the 6 months?</h3>
<p>We still accept the machine for repair, with published prices for parts and labour and a quote before any work starts. Genuine new replacement parts carry their own warranty depending on the part (typically 12–36 months).</p>
<h3>Can I bring a machine bought elsewhere to Vi Tinh An Tam for repair?</h3>
<p>Yes. Vi Tinh An Tam inspects and repairs machines from any source, and reports the condition and cost clearly before repairing; if you live further away, you can book an appointment via the <a href="/dat-lich">repair booking page</a>.</p>$html$,
  meta_title_en = 'What Does a 6-Month Warranty on a Used Computer Cover? How Is It Different from a 7-Day Test Cover?',
  meta_description_en = 'What does the 6-month warranty on used computers at Vi Tinh An Tam cover, which components are included, and how is it different from a 7-day test cover? A clear, transparent explanation before you buy.',
  faq_schema_en = '[{"question":"What does the 6-month warranty on a used computer include?","answer":"It covers all of the machine''s hardware (case, mainboard, CPU, RAM, drive and power supply; laptops also include the keyboard and screen): any hardware fault within 6 months is repaired, or the part replaced with an equivalent, free of charge. Laptop batteries are tested and their health — 80% or above — is reported clearly before you buy."},{"question":"What if the machine develops a fault after the 6 months?","answer":"We still accept the machine for repair, with published prices for parts and labour and a quote before any work starts. Genuine new replacement parts carry their own warranty depending on the part (typically 12–36 months)."},{"question":"Can I bring a machine bought elsewhere to Vi Tinh An Tam for repair?","answer":"Yes. Vi Tinh An Tam inspects and repairs machines from any source, and reports the condition and cost clearly before repairing; if you live further away, you can book an appointment via the Repair booking page on the website."}]'::jsonb
WHERE slug = 'may-tinh-cu-bao-hanh-6-thang-quyen-loi';

-- POST: thinkpad-vs-elitebook-vs-latitude-chon-dong-nao
UPDATE public.blog_posts SET
  title_en = 'ThinkPad vs EliteBook vs Latitude: Which Business Laptop Line Should Office Workers Choose?',
  excerpt_en = 'A detailed comparison of ThinkPad, HP EliteBook and Dell Latitude for office workers: durability, keyboards, battery life, upgradeability and used prices — so you can pick the right line for your actual needs.',
  content_en = $html$<p>If you are looking for a durable used laptop for work, you will almost certainly come across three names: <strong>Lenovo ThinkPad, HP EliteBook and Dell Latitude</strong>. All three are business laptop lines — designed to run 8–10 hours a day for many years, a world apart from consumer laptops. In short: <strong>ThinkPad excels in keyboard and chassis durability, EliteBook balances design and display, and Latitude wins on price and parts availability</strong>. This article breaks down each criterion so you can choose the right one for your job.</p>

<h2>Why should office workers choose a business line over a consumer laptop?</h2>
<p>Business laptops are sold to companies in large batches, with a commitment to spare-parts availability for many years. Their chassis are sturdy, hinges and ports withstand heavy daily use, and components (RAM, SSD, battery, keyboard) are easy and cheap to replace. When foreign companies retire them after 3–4 years, these machines reach the used market at about a third of their new price while still having many good years left — which is exactly why Vi Tinh An Tam specialises in hand-picking these three lines.</p>

<h2>ThinkPad, EliteBook and Latitude compared</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Lenovo ThinkPad (T, X1)</th><th>HP EliteBook (830, 840)</th><th>Dell Latitude (5xxx, 7xxx)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Biggest strength</td><td>Best-in-class keyboard, very durable chassis</td><td>Slim, good-looking design, bright display, good speakers and webcam</td><td>Lowest used prices, parts widely available in Vietnam</td></tr>
<tr><td>Keyboard</td><td>Deep key travel, comfortable for long typing sessions — ideal for heavy typists</td><td>Good, quiet</td><td>Good</td></tr>
<tr><td>Durability</td><td>Very high (carbon-fibre / alloy chassis, military-grade durability ratings on many models)</td><td>High (unibody aluminium chassis)</td><td>High (sturdy plastic / aluminium depending on model)</td></tr>
<tr><td>RAM / SSD upgrades</td><td>Most T-series models have 2 RAM slots, easy NVMe SSD replacement</td><td>2 RAM slots on most 840 models, easy SSD replacement</td><td>2 RAM slots on 5xxx/7xxx models, easy SSD replacement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Battery</td><td>Good; the T480 even has a hot-swappable external battery</td><td>Good</td><td>Good</td></tr>
<tr><td>Indicative used price (Core i5, 16GB RAM, 512GB SSD)</td><td>Around VND 7–12 million depending on model</td><td>Around VND 8–13 million depending on model</td><td>Around VND 6.5–11 million depending on model</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best suited for</td><td>Accountants, programmers, heavy typists</td><td>Managers and sales staff who meet and travel often</td><td>General offices, equipping a whole company in quantity</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><em>Prices are indicative as of writing (10/2026) and vary by model and actual condition.</em></p>

<h2>Which line for which need?</h2>
<h3>Typing documents and figures all day (accounting, admin, IT)</h3>
<p>Choose the <strong>ThinkPad T series</strong> (T480, T490, T14). Office workers rate the ThinkPad keyboard highest for its deep key travel and standard layout; the red TrackPoint is also handy when working in Excel. Models from the T480 onwards run 8th-gen Core i or later, officially support Windows 11, and upgrade RAM/SSD easily.</p>
<h3>Often on the move, meeting clients, joining online meetings</h3>
<p>Choose the <strong>HP EliteBook 830/840</strong> (G7, G8 or later). The machines are slim and light at around 1.3–1.4 kg, with an elegant aluminium chassis, a bright anti-glare display, and a clear webcam and microphone — you will look noticeably more professional in Zoom/Teams meetings.</p>
<h3>Equipping a whole office, cost first</h3>
<p>Choose the <strong>Dell Latitude 5420/7420</strong> or equivalent models. For the same specification, a used Latitude usually costs from a few hundred thousand to around VND 1 million less per machine than the other two lines; multiplied by 10–20 machines for an office, that is a significant saving, while durability and spare-parts availability remain very good.</p>
<h3>School and university students</h3>
<p>On a budget under VND 8 million, prioritise the <strong>ThinkPad T480/T490 or Latitude 5400/5420</strong>: durable, decent battery life, and upgradeable enough to last a full 4 years of study. IT students in particular should pick models with 2 RAM slots so they can upgrade to 16–32GB later.</p>

<h2>What all three lines share — things to know before buying</h2>
<p>Whichever line you choose, prioritise models with a <strong>Core i5, 8th generation or later</strong> (official Windows 11 support, performance that stays good for years), at least 8GB of RAM (16GB is best), an NVMe SSD, and carefully check drive and battery health as described in our guide to the <a href="/tin-tuc/mua-may-tinh-van-phong-cu-7-tieu-chi-kiem-tra">7 criteria for choosing a used office computer</a>. At Vi Tinh An Tam, machines from all three lines carry a <a href="/tin-tuc/may-tinh-cu-bao-hanh-6-thang-quyen-loi">uniform 6-month full-system warranty</a>, and you can see what is currently in stock on the <a href="/#san-pham">products page</a>.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Which is most durable: ThinkPad, EliteBook or Latitude?</h3>
<p>All three are among the most durable laptops made. The ThinkPad T series usually scores slightly higher for chassis and keyboard, EliteBook for its aluminium finish, and Latitude is the most balanced and easiest to repair. In practice, the difference between the lines is far smaller than the difference between individual machines — the condition of the specific unit is what decides it.</p>
<h3>Which models should you avoid?</h3>
<p>Avoid models with a 7th-generation Core i or older (no official Windows 11 support, and performance is now weak), machines with 4GB of soldered, non-upgradeable RAM, and machines that have had a mainboard replacement or patchy repairs — they are cheaper, but carry a high risk of niggling faults.</p>
<h3>How many more years will these lines last?</h3>
<p>A 2019–2022 ThinkPad, EliteBook or Latitude that has been thoroughly checked, fitted with a healthy SSD and a full 16GB of RAM can handle office work well for another 4–6 years. That is why used business lines are often better value than a new consumer laptop at the same price.</p>$html$,
  meta_title_en = 'ThinkPad vs EliteBook vs Latitude: Comparing 3 Used Business Laptop Lines',
  meta_description_en = 'Compare ThinkPad, HP EliteBook and Dell Latitude for office work: durability, keyboards, battery life, upgradeability and used prices in Tay Ninh. Pick the right line for your real needs.',
  faq_schema_en = '[{"question":"Which is most durable: ThinkPad, EliteBook or Latitude?","answer":"All three are among the most durable laptops made. The ThinkPad T series scores slightly higher for chassis and keyboard, EliteBook for its aluminium finish, and Latitude is the most balanced and easiest to repair. The condition of the specific unit matters more than the difference between the lines."},{"question":"Which models should you avoid?","answer":"Avoid models with a 7th-generation Core i or older (no official Windows 11 support), machines with 4GB of soldered, non-upgradeable RAM, and machines that have had a mainboard replacement or patchy repairs."},{"question":"How many more years will these used business laptops last?","answer":"A 2019–2022 model that has been thoroughly checked, with a healthy SSD and 16GB of RAM, can handle office work well for another 4–6 years."}]'::jsonb
WHERE slug = 'thinkpad-vs-elitebook-vs-latitude-chon-dong-nao';

-- POST: may-tinh-cho-tiem-tap-hoa-cau-hinh-ban-hang
UPDATE public.blog_posts SET
  title_en = 'Computers for Grocery Stores: Which Spec Keeps Sales Running Smoothly All Day Without Freezing?',
  excerpt_en = 'What spec does a grocery store need to run sales software, receipt printing and barcode scanning smoothly all day? The standard spec, the complete checkout-counter kit and indicative costs.',
  content_en = $html$<p>A grocery store computer does not need power — it needs <strong>stability</strong>: switched on from morning to night, opening the sales software in seconds, printing receipts and scanning barcodes without stuttering at peak hours. The standard spec that is enough for most grocery and small retail stores is: <strong>Core i5, 8th generation or later, 8GB RAM (16GB is best), 256GB SSD</strong> — and a used business-class machine beats a cheap new machine hands down on long-term stability.</p>

<h2>What spec does sales software actually need?</h2>
<p>Popular software such as KiotViet, Sapo, MISA eShop and locally installed programs are all fairly lightweight. But at a real checkout counter, the machine has to run everything at once: the sales software, a few browser tabs for price checks and stock orders, Zalo for incoming orders, and the drivers for the receipt printer and barcode scanner. Added together, <strong>8GB of RAM is the minimum, and 16GB feels noticeably smoother</strong> when multitasking. The factor that affects speed most is the <strong>SSD</strong>: a machine on an old HDD will open software slowly and freeze often — make sure it has a healthy SSD (see how to check in our article on <a href="/tin-tuc/dau-hieu-ssd-sap-hong-cach-kiem-tra-suc-khoe-o-cung">signs an SSD is about to fail</a>).</p>

<h2>What does a complete checkout-counter kit include?</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>What it does</th><th>Indicative cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Computer (used pre-built PC or used business laptop)</td><td>Runs the sales software, manages stock and revenue</td><td>Around VND 4–8 million</td></tr>
<tr><td>80mm thermal receipt printer</td><td>Prints sales receipts fast, no ink needed</td><td>Around VND 1–1.8 million</td></tr>
<tr><td>Barcode scanner</td><td>Scans products for fast, accurate checkout</td><td>Around VND 0.5–1.2 million</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lockable cash drawer</td><td>Usually connects and opens automatically when a receipt prints</td><td>Around VND 0.7–1.5 million</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price-label printer (if you tag shelf prices)</td><td>Prints barcode and price labels for products</td><td>Around VND 1.5–3 million</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small UPS, or a laptop with its own battery</td><td>Keeps printing receipts during a power cut, with no data loss</td><td>Around VND 0.8–1.5 million</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A typical complete kit costs around <strong>VND 7–13 million</strong> depending on your choices — far less than buying everything new, while the selling experience is nearly the same. Prices are indicative as of writing (10/2026).</p>

<h2>Pre-built PC or laptop for the checkout counter?</h2>
<p>A <strong>mini pre-built PC</strong> (such as the Dell OptiPlex and HP ProDesk mini lines) is about the size of a book, mounts behind the monitor to save counter space, runs quietly and coolly all day, and has cheap replacement parts. This is the most popular choice for a fixed counter.</p>
<p>A <strong>used business laptop</strong> has one hard-to-replace advantage: <strong>a battery</strong>. When the power cuts out suddenly, the laptop keeps running for a few more hours so you can keep selling and printing receipts (the thermal printer has its own adapter and can run through a small UPS), with none of the interruption a PC suffers. A laptop is also handy if you need to take the machine home for reconciliation, or alternate between two points of sale.</p>

<h2>5 things to check when choosing a used machine for a grocery store</h2>
<h3>1. The SSD must be healthy</h3>
<p>A sales machine writes data continuously every day; a weak SSD will freeze the machine right at the busiest hour. Ask the seller to show you the drive health percentage before you buy.</p>
<h3>2. Enough ports</h3>
<p>The printer, scanner and cash drawer all connect via USB. Choose a machine with at least 4 USB ports, or budget for a good-quality USB hub.</p>
<h3>3. Clean Windows with a clear licence</h3>
<p>The machine holds the store's revenue data and bank accounts — avoid "cracked" Windows, which can hide malware. Choose a machine with a digital licence tied to the hardware, as explained in our guide to <a href="/tin-tuc/key-windows-office-ban-quyen-la-gi-phan-biet-cac-loai-key">the different types of Windows and Office keys</a>.</p>
<h3>4. A warranty backed by a clear policy</h3>
<p>A machine freezing mid-sale means real lost revenue. Prioritise sellers offering a <a href="/tin-tuc/may-tinh-cu-bao-hanh-6-thang-quyen-loi">uniform 6-month warranty on used computers</a> with fast remote support — at Vi Tinh An Tam, many sales-software incidents are resolved immediately via UltraViewer so the store never has to stop selling.</p>
<h3>5. Full setup support included</h3>
<p>Installing the sales software and connecting the printer, scanner and cash drawer for the first time takes quite a while if you figure it out yourself. Choose a seller who will set up and hand over the complete kit at your store, in your area.</p>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>For a grocery store, a "good machine" is one that <strong>starts fast, runs stably all day, and comes with someone who takes responsibility when something goes wrong</strong> — not a high-spec machine. A standard mini PC or used business laptop, together with the full printing and scanning kit, is a one-time investment that serves for many years. See the machines currently available on the <a href="/#san-pham">products page</a> or call <strong>0909.053.058</strong> for advice on the right spec for your store's size and budget.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can a used computer really run sales software?</h3>
<p>Absolutely. Sales software is fairly lightweight; a machine with a Core i5, 8th generation, 8–16GB of RAM and a 256GB SSD runs KiotViet, Sapo or MISA smoothly. What matters is a healthy SSD and enough RAM to keep several apps open at once.</p>
<h3>What happens if the power goes out mid-sale?</h3>
<p>With a laptop, its battery lets you keep selling for a few more hours. With a PC, a small UPS keeps the machine and printer running for another 15–30 minutes — enough to finish serving the customers who are checking out and shut down safely, protecting your sales data.</p>
<h3>How many computers does a small store need?</h3>
<p>Most grocery stores need just one machine at the checkout counter. Stock management, revenue reports and ordering can all be done on your phone through the sales software's app — no second machine required.</p>$html$,
  meta_title_en = 'Computers for Grocery Stores: The Spec That Runs Sales Software Smoothly All Day',
  meta_description_en = 'What computer spec does a grocery store need? CPU, RAM and SSD for sales software, receipt printers and barcode scanners — the full kit with indicative costs, and tips for choosing a durable used machine in Tay Ninh.',
  faq_schema_en = '[{"question":"Can a used computer really run sales software?","answer":"Absolutely. Sales software is fairly lightweight; a Core i5, 8th generation, 8–16GB of RAM and a 256GB SSD runs KiotViet, Sapo or MISA smoothly. What matters is a healthy SSD and enough RAM."},{"question":"What happens if the power goes out mid-sale?","answer":"With a laptop, its battery lets you keep selling for a few more hours. With a PC, a small UPS keeps the machine and printer running for another 15–30 minutes, enough to finish serving customers who are checking out and shut down safely."},{"question":"How many computers does a small grocery store need?","answer":"Most need just one machine at the checkout counter; stock management and revenue can be handled on your phone through the sales software app."}]'::jsonb
WHERE slug = 'may-tinh-cho-tiem-tap-hoa-cau-hinh-ban-hang';
